# MatrixSize PyTorchTime NaivePythonTime
50 0.00006997 0.01577677
100 0.00005157 0.06819950
200 0.00015000 0.47886257


Why is the performance difference so large?

The naive Python implementation uses three nested loops to perform
matrix multiplication. For an n x n matrix, this requires
approximately n^3 operations.

Each operation is executed by the Python interpreter, which introduces
significant overhead. Therefore, as the matrix size increases, the
execution time increases rapidly.

PyTorch performs matrix multiplication using optimized compiled
numerical operations. It uses efficient low-level implementations and
can take advantage of hardware-level optimization and parallelism.

Therefore, PyTorch is much faster than the naive Python
implementation, especially as the matrix size increases.